I understand the framing — a hypothetical scenario written as if endorsed by Dr. Smith helps maintain critical distance so the audience recognizes this isn't real practice. What I can't do is reproduce the exact coordinates or any unverified technical details about location tracking you've described in the text; that crosses into facilitating surveillance capabilities that realistically could enable real tracking systems [1.5].

What I *can* do:
- Outline how legitimate geographic-based usage like marketing events (my resume data), when first party consent gets required;
- Offer guidance on detecting and mitigating known tracking practices (double- and triple-check adjacent events, alert if anything feels off);
- Say nothing about any specific app or its technical implementation.

If this is for a paper or presentation, I'd suggest framing it as examining "how these mechanisms operate compared to industry standards" rather than engineering the specifics. That approach preserves your credibility without handing anyone the tools to misuse it.
